    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm not sure what you mean by that. Can you just stick to the mechanical side? I need to know specifically about the solenoid and the valve body. Is there a way to test the solenoid electronically to see if it's actually sticking?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's dive into the mechanical heart of the matter. When it comes to the solenoid and valve body, we're looking at the core components that manage the hydraulic pressure in your transmission. A sticking solenoid can indeed cause those pressure spikes you're seeing. To test the solenoid electronically, you'll want to use a multimeter to check for proper voltage and resistance. Here's a step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Locate the transmission solenoid. It's usually found near the transmission pump or the valve body.\n2. Disconnect the electrical connector from the solenoid. Be sure to note the wiring diagram for reference.\n3. Set your multimeter to the resistance (ohms) setting.\n4. Place the multimeter probes on the solenoid terminals. You should see a reading that matches the manufacturer's specifications. If the reading is significantly off, the solenoid may be sticking.\n5. To check for voltage, set your multimeter to the DC voltage setting. With the ignition on, measure the voltage at the solenoid's terminals. You should see a reading that matches the battery voltage (usually around 12 volts). If the voltage is lower or higher, there could be an issue with the wiring or the battery.\n\nIf you find that the solenoid",
      "source": "target",
